title The red that broke a meter by closing, and the two claims I did not check
prompt approve grant all your recommendations -- reds-first put this ahead of %430.
think Booked before fixing, per reds-first, taking the number from the allocator after a fetch rather than from the ledger's own headline.
think Asked which references crossed the ceiling rather than assuming, and found the tool already answered: dated_path_scan.sh takes a list verb.
think Narrowed by intersecting the 65 citing files of lost references against the files touched in the last 40 commits, which returned two -- construction/REDS.md and tools/fixtures/c/commit_message_guard_scan.sh.
think All three crossing references were one fabricated name, 20260905-183418_the-handoff-from-the-eight-core-round.md, which is the citation %437 booked at 18:36 -- planted once in the control to prove the hook, quoted twice in the ledger row to record the escape.
think Read dated_path_exclusions.sh before adding to it, and found it had already reasoned this case twice: its sixth LISTED entry is the same control's earlier fabricated plant under %202, and its fifth says a ledger row naming a removed page is a path asserted absent rather than a reference gone stale.
obs A red closed correctly at 18:36 broke a different guard's ceiling by the act of closing. The exclusions file's own warning names it exactly: counting them makes a meter rise as its proof gets stronger.
obs Listed both spellings, since the control and the row both abbreviate the name to 20260905-183418_...md on second use and an ellipsis is still a stamp and a sprig to the census. Listing one spelling would have made the subtraction depend on how a sentence happened to be typed. refs_lost 168 -> 165 against a ceiling of 165, under_ceiling=yes, witness GREEN.
obs TWO UNVERIFIED CLAIMS, BOTH MINE, BOTH IN THIS ROUND. First: I wrote in a session log and in commit 2389e419d's body that the guard was unrostered, having grepped a roster PASS's output rather than the roster file, which names it at line 65 at tier cadence. Second: the first draft of the %438 row claimed the census names no reference and owed a listing, and the list verb has existed since the scan was written -- it is how I found the three ten minutes later.
obs That is %436's shape twice, one day after %436 was booked. The difference worth recording is that a peer's finding arrives marked as someone else's and invites a check, where my own reads as something I already know, so nothing in the sentence asks to be checked.
obs The second claim was caught before it shipped and the row was rewritten in the working tree; the first had already been pushed, so it stands in its log and commit body where a superseded claim belongs, and the row is the erratum.
obs The guard I built earlier this round checked my own census headline edit as the row landed -- headline_total_lines=1, duplicate_census_lines=0 -- which is the first thing it has been asked about that was not a test.
obs A cadence tier is not a silence: dated_path speaks the fifth round, so a rise can stand four rounds before anything says so. That is lawful by design and is named here rather than booked.
obs THE ROW ITSELF THEN BROKE THE PIN. At 3,480 bytes the %438 row carried construction/REDS.md to 27,593 against the 24,576 it declares, and two rostered guards said so on the same pass -- declared_ceiling and equinox_e123_living_pin_guard, both reading the same one over-bound pin from different rooms. A red about a meter rising cost the pin its bound, which is the round's own lesson arriving a third time.
obs Folded %436 and %437 to construction/archive/REDS-a-claim-that-escaped-checking-rows-436-437.md through tools/fixtures/r/reds_fold.sh rather than by hand, since that loom exists because the link re-anchoring step was forgotten twice (%247, %270). The pin reads 23,630 now. The two rows belong together and beside the one that stayed: a claim that reads true and is never asked to prove it, escaping a reader in one and a machine in the other.
obs The fold tool refused its first call at verdict=shelf_absent -- a shelf's header is a person's to write, so the tool moves rows and never invents the sentence that says why they belong together. That refusal is the tool being right about the division of labour.
obs tracked_link went RED a second time in one round for the same reason, the new shelf existing on disk and not in the index. Twice is structure rather than carelessness: any lap that writes a new file and cites it is red until it stages, and the guard is stating its whole purpose each time.
loom guard=dated_path refs_lost_before=168 refs_lost_after=165 ceiling=165 listed_spellings=2 citing_files_of_lost=65
file construction/REDS.md %438 booked, then rewritten before it shipped when its own claim failed a check
file tools/fixtures/d/dated_path_exclusions.sh both spellings of the fabricated name LISTED, with the reasoning written beside the two entries that had already settled it
file construction/ITINERARY.md the live front carries %438's two lessons
file session-logs/date/20260905/20260905-195223_the-red-that-broke-a-meter-by-closing.kyri this record
recommend keep-going -- %430 next, whose fix Keaton granted: free the Register share only where one sentence could carry it across the ceiling.
